%[text] # Week 9 Lecture Demonstration: Fitting Ohm's Law Data
%[text] **LECTURE DEMONSTRATION**
%[text] PHY4605 Physics-First Reproducible MATLAB Notebook
%%
%[text] ## Physical Question and Learning Outcomes
%[text] A resistor is labelled 47 ohm, but individual voltage-current measurements are not perfectly exact. How can a fitted straight line estimate the resistance, show where the model misses the measurements, and support a cautious statement about uncertainty? We will fit a supplied physical relationship, interpret parameter units, inspect residuals, and validate the result.
%%
%[text] ## Predict Before Computing
%[text] Ohm's law predicts that voltage rises approximately in proportion to current for a resistor whose resistance is nearly constant. Before fitting, predict the sign and approximate magnitude of the slope on a graph of voltage against current. A 47 ohm resistor should increase by about 0.47 V for every 0.01 A increase in current.
%%
%[text] ## Model, Measurements, and Units
%[text] The proposed measurement model is $V=RI+b$. Here current $I$ is measured in amperes, voltage $V$ is measured in volts, the fitted slope $R$ has unit V/A = ohm, and the fitted intercept $b$ has unit volts. The small intercept allows a possible zero offset in the measurement system. The measured current and voltage values are data; resistance and offset are parameters inferred from the whole dataset.
I_A = (0.01:0.01:0.08)';
V_measured_V = [0.48 0.93 1.43 1.86 2.36 2.83 3.28 3.77]';
measurement_table = table(I_A,V_measured_V, ... %[output:group:623b5195] %[output:9e8c1e08]
    'VariableNames',{'Current_A','MeasuredVoltage_V'}) %[output:group:623b5195] %[output:9e8c1e08]
%%
%[text] ## Algorithm Before MATLAB
%[text] - Plot the measured current-voltage points before fitting.
%[text] - State the proposed relationship and identify the parameter units.
%[text] - Use the supplied straight-line fitting scaffold to obtain slope and intercept.
%[text] - Evaluate the fitted model at the measured current values and overlay it on the data.
%[text] - Calculate residual = measured voltage - fitted voltage.
%[text] - Inspect the residual pattern, then validate the resistance against a known reference or plausible range.
%[text] - Report the fitted resistance with only the precision justified by the supplied repeatability evidence. \
%%
%[text] ## Plot the Data Before Fitting
%[text] A fit should answer a physical question about visible data. Plot the measurements first so an obviously curved trend, outlier, or unit mistake is not hidden by a fitting command.
plot(I_A,V_measured_V,'o','MarkerSize',7,'LineWidth',1.4) %[output:7bfe93d4]
xlabel('Current, I (A)') %[output:7bfe93d4]
ylabel('Voltage, V (V)') %[output:7bfe93d4]
title('Measured Voltage-Current Data') %[output:7bfe93d4]
grid on %[output:7bfe93d4]
%%
%[text] ## Fit a Straight Line With a Supplied Scaffold
%[text] `polyfit(x,y,1)` returns the coefficients of a first-degree polynomial. For this model, the first coefficient is the slope in V/A and the second is the intercept in V. The fitting calculation uses all eight measurements rather than choosing one pair of points.
fit_coefficients = polyfit(I_A,V_measured_V,1);
R_fit_ohm = fit_coefficients(1) %[output:4dc6fd22]
offset_fit_V = fit_coefficients(2) %[output:429d000a]
assert(abs(R_fit_ohm-47) < 0.1)
%%
%[text] ## Interpret the Fitted Parameters
%[text] The fitted slope is 47.0000 V/A, so the resistance estimate is 47.0000 ohm. The fitted intercept is 0.0025 V, which is small compared with the measured voltages. Do not call the slope a measured current or voltage: it is a parameter inferred from how the full dataset changes.
parameter_summary = table(R_fit_ohm,offset_fit_V, ... %[output:group:2f38186a] %[output:9eca3fb5]
    'VariableNames',{'FittedResistance_ohm','FittedOffset_V'}) %[output:group:2f38186a] %[output:9eca3fb5]
%%
%[text] ## Overlay the Fitted Model and Measurements
%[text] `polyval` evaluates the fitted straight line at chosen current values. The dense current array makes a smooth visual line; the residual calculation later uses predictions at the original measured currents.
I_line_A = linspace(min(I_A),max(I_A),200);
V_line_V = polyval(fit_coefficients,I_line_A);
plot(I_A,V_measured_V,'o',I_line_A,V_line_V,'-','LineWidth',1.6) %[output:2e9e86e2]
xlabel('Current, I (A)') %[output:2e9e86e2]
ylabel('Voltage, V (V)') %[output:2e9e86e2]
title('Measured Data and Fitted Ohm-Law Model') %[output:2e9e86e2]
legend('Measurements','V = R I + b fit','Location','northwest') %[output:2e9e86e2]
grid on %[output:2e9e86e2]
%%
%[text] ## Residual Means Measured Minus Model
%[text] A residual is $r\_i=V\_{i,measured}-V\_{i,fit}$. Positive residuals lie above the fitted line; negative residuals lie below it. Residuals are in volts because they are voltage differences. A useful residual plot should look like small scatter around zero rather than a clear curve or one dominating point.
V_fit_at_data_V = polyval(fit_coefficients,I_A);
residual_V = V_measured_V-V_fit_at_data_V;
residual_RMSE_V = sqrt(mean(residual_V.^2)) %[output:293f822a]
largest_absolute_residual_V = max(abs(residual_V)) %[output:814a63c9]
residual_table = table(I_A,V_measured_V,V_fit_at_data_V,residual_V, ... %[output:group:0fa6a43a] %[output:01cbdace]
    'VariableNames',{'Current_A','MeasuredVoltage_V','FittedVoltage_V','Residual_V'}) %[output:group:0fa6a43a] %[output:01cbdace]
plot(I_A,residual_V,'o-','LineWidth',1.3) %[output:0d0d072d]
yline(0,'--') %[output:0d0d072d]
xlabel('Current, I (A)') %[output:0d0d072d]
ylabel('Residual, measured - fitted (V)') %[output:0d0d072d]
title('Residuals for the Straight-Line Fit') %[output:0d0d072d]
grid on %[output:0d0d072d]
assert(abs(mean(residual_V)) < 1e-12)
%[text] The residuals alternate around zero and stay within about 0.023 V. There is no obvious curved pattern in this small teaching dataset. That supports the straight-line relationship over this measured range, but it does not prove that resistance is constant under every operating condition.
%%
%[text] ## Validate Against a Physical Reference
%[text] The component is labelled 47 ohm with a 5 percent nominal tolerance. That manufacturer range is 44.65 to 49.35 ohm. The fitted resistance lies inside it. This is a useful physical/reference check, but component tolerance is not an uncertainty estimate for the fitting procedure.
R_nominal_ohm = 47;
R_tolerance_fraction = 0.05;
R_nominal_range_ohm = R_nominal_ohm*[1-R_tolerance_fraction 1+R_tolerance_fraction] %[output:3fe39412]
fit_within_nominal_tolerance = R_fit_ohm >= R_nominal_range_ohm(1) && ... %[output:group:8f557177] %[output:68a32638]
    R_fit_ohm <= R_nominal_range_ohm(2) %[output:group:8f557177] %[output:68a32638]
assert(fit_within_nominal_tolerance)
%%
%[text] ## State Uncertainty Cautiously
%[text] Three supplied repeat runs of the same measurement procedure give fitted slopes close to 47 ohm. Use their observed span as a simple repeatability range. This is deliberately a modest Week 9 statement: it is not a formal confidence interval and does not include every possible source of systematic error.
V_repeat1_V = V_measured_V;
V_repeat2_V = [0.47 0.95 1.40 1.89 2.34 2.84 3.31 3.75]';
V_repeat3_V = [0.46 0.94 1.42 1.87 2.37 2.81 3.30 3.78]';
repeat_data_V = [V_repeat1_V V_repeat2_V V_repeat3_V];
R_repeat_ohm = zeros(1,size(repeat_data_V,2));
for k = 1:size(repeat_data_V,2)
    repeat_fit = polyfit(I_A,repeat_data_V(:,k),1);
    R_repeat_ohm(k) = repeat_fit(1);
end
R_repeatability_range_ohm = [min(R_repeat_ohm) max(R_repeat_ohm)] %[output:88e55c31]
%[text] The repeat fitted slopes are approximately 47.00, 47.06, and 47.27 ohm. A cautious classroom statement is: "the fitted resistance is about 47.1 ohm, with repeat runs spanning roughly 47.0 to 47.3 ohm." Do not add more decimal places than the repeatability evidence supports.
%%
%[text] ## Recognise a Structured Residual Pattern
%[text] Residuals can reveal a model problem even when a fitted line looks visually plausible. The supplied diagnostic pattern below is not another fit to master. If low-current residuals are positive, middle residuals negative, and high-current residuals positive again, the U-shaped structure suggests that a straight line is missing curvature or another physical effect. The next action is to question the model or experiment, not automatically delete data.
I_diagnostic_A = (0.01:0.01:0.08)';
structured_residual_V = [0.06 0.02 -0.03 -0.05 -0.04 -0.01 0.03 0.07]';
plot(I_diagnostic_A,structured_residual_V,'o-','LineWidth',1.3) %[output:70ab56cd]
yline(0,'--') %[output:70ab56cd]
xlabel('Current, I (A)') %[output:70ab56cd]
ylabel('Residual (V)') %[output:70ab56cd]
title('Example of Residual Structure That Needs Investigation') %[output:70ab56cd]
grid on %[output:70ab56cd]
%%
%[text] ## Working Exposure: Read a Supplied Fit-Quality Statistic
%[text] The coefficient of determination $R^2$ is supplied for interpretation only. Values closer to 1 mean that the fitted predictions account for more of the observed variation in this dataset, but a high value does not replace residual inspection, physical units, or model judgement.
SS_res = sum(residual_V.^2);
SS_tot = sum((V_measured_V-mean(V_measured_V)).^2);
R_squared = 1-SS_res/SS_tot %[output:438fbade]
%[text] For this dataset the value is close to 1. Treat that as supporting evidence alongside the residual plot and physical reference, not as a guarantee that the model is universally correct.
%%
%[text] ## Physical Interpretation and Reproducibility
%[text] A fit converts the pattern across many measured points into parameter estimates. Parameter units come from the physical equation, residuals show where the model misses individual measurements, and uncertainty statements must match the evidence actually available. Here the straight-line model is adequate over the supplied current range and gives a resistance consistent with the nominal 47 ohm component.
%[text] Run from top to bottom in a fresh MATLAB session with MATLAB R2025a or later and base MATLAB. The locked current-voltage data, nominal resistance, tolerance, repeat runs, fitting commands, residual checks, and uncertainty wording are all supplied in this file.

